{0 Bincaml Language}


{1 Primitive Operations}

All the operations are defined in Ops.ml. There are some categories.


{2 Partial Operations}

Bincaml is a total language, meaning that partial operations like division by
zero are infeasible.

Some conditional constructs are implemented by a chocie over a set of partial functions,
by executing them in order and taking the first that returns. Therefore,
the choice construct must be total.

The partial operations consist of:

{ul
{- [IfThen cond value]: does not return if cond is false. }
{- [Pattern VariantCase value handlefunction]: calls [handlefunction(value)] if
   [value] is of case [VariantCase]. [handlefunction] itself may also be
   partial.  }
{- Divisions by zero: [IntDIV] and [BVDIV] }
{- Forall and Exists in an evaluation context }
}


{1 Types}


{2 Memory Layout}

Types which have a defined memory layout are:

{ul
    {- Bitvectors }
    {- Booleans }
    {- Records containing only layoutable types. }
}

To implement this we define the primitive ops:

[ToBitvector] which converts a layoutable type to an equal sized bitvector, and

[FromBitvector type] which converts a bitvector to [type] if it is layoutable.



{2 Notable Exclusions}

{3 Polymorphism}

SMTLib datatypes take type parameters, allowing an initial generic declaration with subsequent
concrete instantiations. While we have parametric type constructors, we only allow declaring a concrete
type.
